  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nA robust mid/upper low is centered over Manitoba, with \ndry/subsident air centered over our CWA on WV imagery and 925 \nMB CAA is spreading into our CWA from the west/northwest. An \nembedded shortwave within the flow on the back side of the upper \nlow passes through the region later tonight and Wednesday \nmorning an may bring a period of scattered light \nshowers/sprinkles (wetting rain is not expected). Shortwave \nridging followed by west-southwest flow aloft returns mid to \nlate week, with even stronger southerly flow Friday. This brings\nwell above average temperatures back to the region during the \nlater part of the week.\n\nA strong cold front arrives Friday and a series of troughs and \nupper lows are shown to pass through our region with falling \nheights aloft, which should result in much more Fall-like \ntemperatures returning (highs in the 60s). There is a strong \nsignal for rain with the trough passage Sunday night-Monday \nnight and NBM probabilities already show 40-60% chance for \ngreater than 0.5\" rainfall during that period across our CWA \n(highest chances in the southern Red River Valley). That is a \nhopeful signal for those desperate for a soaking rain in the \nsouth, however there is still enough variance in strength of the\nupper low between ensemble clusters and mesoscale details \ncreating uncertainty in where the most beneficial rainfall (1\"+)\nmay ultimately fall. \n\n...Windy Conditions Today...\n\nA combination of deep unidirectional westerly flow through the \nmixed layer, subsidence aloft, and clearing/deeper mixing have \nresulting in many locations across northeast ND gusting 45 to 50 \nmph, with the highest gusts over the Devils Lake Basin. Some \nlocations outside of the advisory are also gusting to 40 mph and\ndue to the depth of mixing occurring I would not be surprised \nif a few gusts to 45 mph could occur farther south towards I-94 \nin southeast ND (chances remaining higher north in the advised \narea). The strongest gusts should wane as low levels begin to \ndecouple towards sunset, and the expiration time of 7pm still \nlooks reasonable. \n\n...Severe Risk Friday Afternoon and Evening...\n\nStrong southerly flow should bring higher BL moisture back to the \nregion, while strong westerly flow aloft overspreads the region\nas a trough passes along with a strong cold front. In the warm\nsector ahead of the front there is the potential for at least \nmoderate CAPE values as steep mid level lapse rates will be in \nplace, though capping may play a role in delayed initiation \nuntil the front and better synoptic ascent arrive. Timing of the\nfront and forcing and any capping ahead of this will all play \nroles in determining coverage and ultimately impacts. Strong \neffective shear is forecast across the region, with hodographs \nin the warm sector supportive of supercells and very large hail \nor tornadoes (mesoscale details will ultimately play a role on \nhow this pattern is realized). AI based systems that have shown \ngood skill this season highlight higher probabilities for severe\nthunderstorms in our area, especially over west central MN \nwhich conceptually makes sense.",
